IMPORT prelude

§ `maximum, maximum1, minimum, minimum1`

-- Regression coverage for the four extremum functions in the prelude, over
-- BOTH of their overloads: `NUMBER` and `MAYBE NUMBER`.
--
-- The two families are copy-and-edit clones of each other, and the `MAYBE`
-- copies of `maximum`/`maximum1` were once left computing minima (an
-- inconsistent clone update). Pinning only the overload that broke would
-- leave its three siblings unpinned, which is how the defect survived, so all
-- four are asserted here for both overloads.
--
-- The inputs are deliberately unsorted, with the extremum in the interior of
-- the list rather than at either end, so an implementation that returns the
-- head, the last element, or the seed cannot pass by accident.

§§ `NUMBER`

#EVAL maximum (LIST 3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6)
#ASSERT maximum (LIST 3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6) EQUALS 9
#ASSERT maximum (LIST 7) EQUALS 7
#ASSERT maximum (LIST -3, -1, -4) EQUALS -1

#EVAL minimum (LIST 3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6)
#ASSERT minimum (LIST 3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6) EQUALS 1
#ASSERT minimum (LIST 7) EQUALS 7
#ASSERT minimum (LIST -3, -1, -4) EQUALS -4

-- maximum1/minimum1 take a seed. Three shapes: the seed loses, the seed wins,
-- and the list is empty (the only case in which the seed is returned unchanged).
#EVAL maximum1 3 (LIST 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6)
#ASSERT maximum1 3 (LIST 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6) EQUALS 9
#ASSERT maximum1 99 (LIST 1, 4, 1, 5) EQUALS 99
#ASSERT maximum1 42 EMPTY EQUALS 42

#EVAL minimum1 3 (LIST 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6)
#ASSERT minimum1 3 (LIST 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6) EQUALS 1
#ASSERT minimum1 -99 (LIST 1, 4, 1, 5) EQUALS -99
#ASSERT minimum1 42 EMPTY EQUALS 42

§§ `MAYBE NUMBER`

#EVAL maximum (LIST (JUST 3), (JUST 1), (JUST 4), (JUST 1), (JUST 5), (JUST 9), (JUST 2), (JUST 6))
#ASSERT maximum (LIST (JUST 3), (JUST 1), (JUST 4), (JUST 1), (JUST 5), (JUST 9), (JUST 2), (JUST 6)) EQUALS JUST 9
#ASSERT maximum (LIST (JUST 7)) EQUALS JUST 7

#EVAL minimum (LIST (JUST 3), (JUST 1), (JUST 4), (JUST 1), (JUST 5), (JUST 9), (JUST 2), (JUST 6))
#ASSERT minimum (LIST (JUST 3), (JUST 1), (JUST 4), (JUST 1), (JUST 5), (JUST 9), (JUST 2), (JUST 6)) EQUALS JUST 1
#ASSERT minimum (LIST (JUST 7)) EQUALS JUST 7

#EVAL maximum1 (JUST 3) (LIST (JUST 1), (JUST 4), (JUST 1), (JUST 5), (JUST 9), (JUST 2), (JUST 6))
#ASSERT maximum1 (JUST 3) (LIST (JUST 1), (JUST 4), (JUST 1), (JUST 5), (JUST 9), (JUST 2), (JUST 6)) EQUALS JUST 9
#ASSERT maximum1 (JUST 99) (LIST (JUST 1), (JUST 4), (JUST 1), (JUST 5)) EQUALS JUST 99
#ASSERT maximum1 (JUST 42) EMPTY EQUALS JUST 42

#EVAL minimum1 (JUST 3) (LIST (JUST 1), (JUST 4), (JUST 1), (JUST 5), (JUST 9), (JUST 2), (JUST 6))
#ASSERT minimum1 (JUST 3) (LIST (JUST 1), (JUST 4), (JUST 1), (JUST 5), (JUST 9), (JUST 2), (JUST 6)) EQUALS JUST 1
#ASSERT minimum1 (JUST 1) (LIST (JUST 4), (JUST 5)) EQUALS JUST 1
#ASSERT minimum1 (JUST 42) EMPTY EQUALS JUST 42

§§ `Pairwise max and min`

#ASSERT max 3 5 EQUALS 5
#ASSERT min 3 5 EQUALS 3
#ASSERT max (JUST 3) (JUST 5) EQUALS JUST 5
#ASSERT min (JUST 3) (JUST 5) EQUALS JUST 3

§§ `NOTHING means absent, not incomparable`

-- The MAYBE overloads of `max`/`min` pattern-match on their arguments rather
-- than comparing them with `<=`/`>=`. That distinction is the whole point of
-- this section: comparison against NOTHING is FALSE (including
-- NOTHING <= NOTHING), so a comparison-based `min` treated NOTHING as winning
-- every comparison and then let a later JUST displace it — which made
-- `minimum` over a list containing NOTHING return neither the minimum nor
-- NOTHING, but whichever JUST happened to come last.
--
-- Under the pattern-matching definitions NOTHING reads as "no value here" and
-- the other operand wins, so an extremum over a list skips the NOTHINGs and is
-- no longer order-dependent. A list with nothing left to compare — empty, or
-- all NOTHING — yields NOTHING.
--
-- Both #EVAL and #ASSERT are kept: the #EVALs keep any future change to MAYBE
-- ordering visible as a golden diff, and the #ASSERTs make a regression fail
-- the suite outright rather than only churning a golden.

#EVAL max (JUST 3) NOTHING
#EVAL max NOTHING (JUST 3)
#EVAL min (JUST 3) NOTHING
#EVAL min NOTHING (JUST 3)
#ASSERT max (JUST 3) NOTHING EQUALS JUST 3
#ASSERT max NOTHING (JUST 3) EQUALS JUST 3
#ASSERT min (JUST 3) NOTHING EQUALS JUST 3
#ASSERT min NOTHING (JUST 3) EQUALS JUST 3

-- The interior NOTHING is the case that was wrong: `minimum` returned JUST 5,
-- the last JUST in the list, rather than JUST 3.
#EVAL maximum (LIST (JUST 3), NOTHING, (JUST 5))
#EVAL minimum (LIST (JUST 3), NOTHING, (JUST 5))
#ASSERT maximum (LIST (JUST 3), NOTHING, (JUST 5)) EQUALS JUST 5
#ASSERT minimum (LIST (JUST 3), NOTHING, (JUST 5)) EQUALS JUST 3

-- NOTHING in every other position, so no single placement can pass by accident.
#ASSERT minimum (LIST NOTHING, (JUST 3), (JUST 5)) EQUALS JUST 3
#ASSERT minimum (LIST (JUST 3), (JUST 5), NOTHING) EQUALS JUST 3
#ASSERT maximum (LIST NOTHING, (JUST 3), (JUST 5)) EQUALS JUST 5
#ASSERT maximum (LIST (JUST 3), (JUST 5), NOTHING) EQUALS JUST 5

-- Nothing left to compare. The empty list needs a typed binding: bare EMPTY
-- does not say which `minimum` overload is meant.
GIVETH A LIST OF MAYBE NUMBER
`no numbers` MEANS EMPTY

GIVETH A LIST OF MAYBE NUMBER
`all nothing` MEANS LIST NOTHING, NOTHING

#EVAL minimum `no numbers`
#EVAL minimum `all nothing`
#ASSERT minimum `no numbers` EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT maximum `no numbers` EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT minimum `all nothing` EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT maximum `all nothing` EQUALS NOTHING
